      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124472/assets/41c25496c3ac1c8cc144aa01019f1cc449f7bf03610703b6c66fbefa19792cdb.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graph III, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, this graph shows a one-to-one function. As a result, this function is invertible.\n\n- For graphs I, II, IV, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. These are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.",
